! The `ph.x` input that generated `reference.out.ph-o2-fixed-lsda`.
!
!   pw.x -in o2-fixed-lsda.in && ph.x -in o2-fixed-lsda.ph.in
!
! `epsil = .true.` with `trans = .false.`: the dielectric constant alone, on a
! spin-polarized ground state. `ph.x` allows LSDA here -- `phq_readin.f90:546`
! refuses an electric field only for *noncollinear* magnetism, and
! `phq_readin.f90:957` only for a smeared or tetrahedron metal -- so this is a
! real Fortran reference for the nspin = 2 dielectric response, not a
! finite difference.
dielectric constant of triplet O2, LSDA
 &inputph
  tr2_ph = 1.0d-14,
  epsil = .true.,
  trans = .false.,
  fildyn = 'o2.dyn',
  amass(1) = 15.99994,
 /
0.0 0.0 0.0
!
! It gives diag(1.110915996, 1.110915996, 1.198004867). defumat cannot yet
! reproduce it, and the reason is neither the occupied count nor anything in
! `defumat/response/`: `dv_of_drho` is one `jvp` of `v_of_rho`, so for
! nspin = 2 it is the *second* derivative of the LSDA exchange-correlation
! energy in the two channel densities, and that diverges wherever a channel
! density reaches zero -- which a plane-wave magnetization does in vacuum.
! 1504 of this cell's 91125 grid points have |m| >= |n|, and `dv_of_drho` has
! exactly 1504 NaN. `chi_0` on the same cell is validated against a central
! difference of the density to 1.1e-6. The reference is committed now so that
! it is there the day the kernel is made twice differentiable at a fully
! polarized point.
